                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Capsular is a very simple arena shooter that is built around one really important rule. One shot kills. That sounds basic, but it changes the whole way I play because I cannot rely on having a big health bar or taking a few hits while I get my aim together. I have to aim properly, watch where the projectiles are going and keep moving. The matches are fast and the controls are easy to understand, so I can get into a game without having to learn a huge amount first.\n\nThe movement is probably my favourite part. I can slide and wall jump around the arenas, which gives the combat a lot more speed than the simple graphics might suggest. Most weapons also use slower projectiles rather than instant hits, so dodging is just as important as shooting. I like that because getting a kill is not only about having better aim. I can watch where a projectile is going, move out of the way and try to catch the other player from a different angle. It makes even a basic weapon feel useful when I know how to use the movement properly.\n\nThere are several weapons to use, including things like the revolver, shotgun, SMG and railgun, and the game also has different maps and modes. I like having enough weapons to change how I approach a fight without the game becoming overloaded with equipment. There is also bot support, which is important for me because it means I can still practise the movement and shooting without needing a full match of people. The four player cooperative content adds another option as well, although the main appeal is definitely the quick arena combat.\n\nThe cosmetic system is handled in a way that I appreciate too. The unlockable items do not give me better weapons or extra combat power, so the actual matches stay focused on what I can do with the basic gameplay. There are hats and other cosmetic items to work towards, but I never feel like I need them to compete. The low poly visuals also suit the game because everything is easy to see and the simple presentation keeps the focus on movement and projectiles. It is not trying to impress me with huge environments or complicated effects.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is that Capsular feels like a game that had a lot more potential than content. The developer officially ended active development when the game left early access in 2019, although an offline mode was added and the developer said the backend would stay online for as long as possible. That means I am looking at a finished game that is also very much from its time. The basic combat is still fun, but there is not a huge amount of depth around it to keep me interested for long periods. I can see myself enjoying a few matches and then moving on rather than spending hours trying to master every part of it.\n\nThe online side is also something I have to be careful about when judging it today. Capsular was designed around multiplayer, but it is an old free game and the developer has not been actively developing it for years. There is offline functionality and bots, which helps a lot, but I would not treat it like a modern competitive shooter with a constantly supported multiplayer scene. That is not really a fault with the core design, but it does affect how much value I can get from the game now. The actual shooting and movement still have a good foundation, but the overall package feels limited.\n\nOverall, Capsular is a fun little arena shooter with a genuinely good central mechanic. I like the one shot kills, projectile based weapons, sliding, wall jumping, simple visuals and the fact that cosmetics do not affect combat. The biggest issue is that there is not enough surrounding the core gameplay to make it feel like a deep long term shooter, and its age is much more noticeable now that active development has ended. Still, when I am actually in a match, the simple rules make every shot matter and the movement gives the combat more personality than I expected. I think it is a good example of a small multiplayer game with a strong idea that could have gone much further.\n\nRating: 7.4/10\n",
